const e={note:"Sanskrit’s currents resist two standard distortions. The first is treating the whole tradition as one thing: what is gathered here spans three thousand years, several religions and a dozen genres that disagree with one another, and where a system is one among many it is named as one among many. The second is reading the material backwards through twentieth-century Western yoga — the familiar seven-cakra scheme is one arrangement among several and reached English largely through Arthur Avalon’s 1919 translation of a sixteenth-century text, which the currents say plainly. Datings are given in both registers: the tradition’s reckoning and the philological one, which for the Veda differ by more than a thousand years, and the gap is itself part of the subject. Every tradition described here is living, and is written about in the present tense.",themes:[{slug:"vedic-recitation",title:"The Woven Recitations",native:"वेदपाठ",translit:"vedapāṭha",summary:"Eleven ways of saying the same words in different orders — a redundancy engineered so that no syllable can move without the error announcing itself.",paragraphs:["The problem was this. Some ten thousand six hundred verses of the Ṛgveda, in a language already archaic to its transmitters, had to be carried without alteration by people who had decided not to write them down — and the decision was principled, since a text whose power lies in its sound is not a text that a written copy can preserve. The answer was a division of labour among schools. Each śākhā, branch, held its own recension and its own supporting literature; the Caraṇavyūha counts 1,131 of them, twenty-one for the Ṛgveda, a hundred and one for the Yajus, a thousand nominally for the Sāman, nine for the Atharvan. Two base texts stood at the bottom of the apparatus: the saṃhitāpāṭha, the continuous chant with all its euphonic fusions in place, and the padapāṭha ascribed to Śākalya, which separates every word from its neighbours and undoes the sandhi — so that the joined and the disjoined forms of the same passage are both memorised, and each is a check on the other.","Above these stand the vikṛtis, the modified recitations. The kramapāṭha is the base of them: words are spoken in overlapping pairs — ab, bc, cd — so that every word is heard twice, in both directions of context. A traditional verse names eight woven patterns built on the krama: jaṭā, mālā, śikhā, rekhā, dhvaja, daṇḍa, ratha and ghana. The jaṭā, the braid, takes each pair forward, backward and forward again: ab, ba, ab; bc, cb, bc. The ghana, the dense or solid, extends the fold across three words: ab, ba, abc, cba, abc. A reciter of the ghanapāṭha therefore utters each word something like thirteen times, in different neighbourhoods and in both directions, and the recitation of a passage runs to many times the length of the passage. What that buys is a property a modern engineer would recognise: a syllable dropped or transposed collides with its own occurrence elsewhere in the weave, and the error is audible at once rather than inherited by the next generation.","What is preserved is not only the sequence of consonants and vowels but the pitch. Vedic Sanskrit had a musical accent — udātta raised, anudātta lowered, svarita falling — and it was phonemic: the accent alone distinguishes bráhman, the sacred formulation, from brahmán, the priest who holds it, and the Taittirīya Saṃhitā's story of the misaccented indraśatru turns on nothing else. The Śikṣā treatises govern articulation, and the Prātiśākhyas — one for each branch, the Ṛgveda's ascribed to Śaunaka, the Vājasaneyi's to Kātyāyana — codify the euphonic rules of that school with a precision no European phonetics reached before the nineteenth century. The Pāṇinīya Śikṣā's most quoted verse tells the reciter to handle the sounds as a tigress carries her cubs in her teeth, gripping them firmly enough that they cannot fall and lightly enough that they are not pierced. Manuscripts later marked the accents with underlines and small vertical strokes, but the marking records the recitation; it does not replace it.","The Sāmaveda posed a different problem, since its business is melody. Its verses are largely borrowed from the Ṛgveda and then stretched, repeated, interrupted and lengthened for singing, with meaningless carrier syllables — the stobhas, hāu, hoyi, huvā — inserted into them, so that the sung text and the verse it comes from can differ almost past recognition. The song-books, the Grāmageyagāna and Āraṇyageyagāna and the two Ūha collections, transmit the melodies rather than the words. Of its schools the Kauthuma and Rāṇāyanīya survive in the north and west and the Jaiminīya in Kerala and Tamil Nadu, and the Jaiminīya melodies differ from the others enough that the divergence is itself evidence about how long the branches have been apart. Frits Staal's Nambudiri Veda Recitation (1961) documented the Kerala tradition in detail and is the closest thing to a technical description of the practice from inside.","The claim the tradition makes for this machinery is that the text has not changed, and it is a claim that can be partly tested, since independent recitation lineages separated for centuries can be compared against each other and against the earliest manuscripts. Scholarship has broadly conceded the point for the Ṛgveda saṃhitā: its phonemes and its accents come down in better condition than many texts transmitted in writing a thousand years later. The concession is narrower than it is often reported to be. It is a statement about one text in one recension, not about the whole Vedic corpus, whose Brāhmaṇa and Sūtra portions show the ordinary variation of any long tradition. Of the 1,131 branches the Caraṇavyūha lists, roughly a dozen survive as living recitation; UNESCO proclaimed the tradition a masterpiece of the oral heritage of humanity in 2003, which is the sort of recognition a practice receives when it has become rare enough to be noticed."],terms:[{native:"संहितापाठ",translit:"saṃhitāpāṭha",gloss:"the continuous recitation, with all euphonic fusions in place; the text as chanted, and the form in which the Veda is liturgically used"},{native:"पदपाठ",translit:"padapāṭha",gloss:"the word-by-word recitation, ascribed to Śākalya, which undoes every sandhi; older than Pāṇini, who refers to it, and the analytic key to the continuous text"},{native:"क्रमपाठ",translit:"kramapāṭha",gloss:"the step recitation, in overlapping pairs — ab, bc, cd — so that each word is heard in both of its contexts; the base on which the woven patterns are built"},{native:"घनपाठ",translit:"ghanapāṭha",gloss:"the dense recitation, folding three words back and forth (ab, ba, abc, cba, abc); the most redundant of the eight vikṛtis, and the one by which a ghanapāṭhin is titled"},{native:"शिक्षा",translit:"śikṣā",gloss:"phonetics; the first of the six Vedāṅgas, treating articulation, quantity and accent — in this tradition a part of scripture rather than a preliminary to it"},{native:"उदात्त",translit:"udātta",gloss:"the raised pitch accent, opposed to anudātta and svarita; phonemic in Vedic, so that a misplaced accent changes what a verse says"}],texts:[{title:"Ṛgveda Prātiśākhya, ascribed to Śaunaka",note:"The phonetic and euphonic rules of the Ṛgvedic recitation, school by school and sound by sound; the technical foundation on which the recitation patterns rest."},{title:"Pāṇinīya Śikṣā",note:"A short verse treatise on articulation attached to Pāṇini's name; the source of the tigress-and-cubs image for how a reciter should hold the sounds."},{title:"Caraṇavyūha",note:"The catalogue of the Vedic branches, counting 1,131 śākhās; a list of what once existed, and so a measure of what has been lost."},{title:"Louis Renou, Les écoles védiques et la formation du Veda (1947)",note:"The standard reconstruction of the school system and of how the recensions came to differ; still the point of entry to the question."},{title:"Frits Staal, Nambudiri Veda Recitation (1961)",note:"A technical description of a living Kerala tradition — pitch, tempo, the vikṛti patterns as actually performed — made before the field had much interest in performance."}]},{slug:"the-sacrifice",title:"The Sacrifice",native:"यज्ञ",translit:"yajña",summary:"A fire altar shaped like a falcon, a brick-count that is a calendar, the geometry that came out of building it, and a twentieth-century argument about whether any of it means anything.",paragraphs:["The śrauta system is the public, large-scale ritual of the Veda, as against the domestic rites of the Gṛhyasūtras. Its basic apparatus is three fires and a patron, the yajamāna, who pays for the rite and receives its fruit; its personnel, for a soma sacrifice, are sixteen officiants under four chiefs — the hotṛ who recites from the Ṛgveda, the adhvaryu who performs the physical operations and murmurs from the Yajus, the udgātṛ who sings from the Sāman, and the brahman who watches in silence and corrects. Its manuals are the Śrautasūtras, one to each branch: Baudhāyana and Āpastamba for the Taittirīyas, Kātyāyana for the Vājasaneyins, Āśvalāyana and Śāṅkhāyana for the Ṛgvedins, Lāṭyāyana for the Sāmavedins. These are engineering documents — measurements, sequences, materials, contingencies — and the Brāhmaṇas beside them are the commentary that says what each act stands for. Nothing about the arrangement is casual: the rites run from the twice-daily agnihotra of a few minutes to celebrations occupying a year.","The Agnicayana, the piling of the fire, is the most elaborate of them. A great altar is built up in five layers from moulded and fired bricks, laid in a prescribed order with a mantra for each, in the shape of a falcon with outstretched wings — the śyenaciti, the bird that carries the sacrificer to heaven. The texts' arithmetic makes the full altar 10,800 bricks, one for each muhūrta of the year, and the sixth to tenth books of the Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa are given over to reading the structure as Prajāpati, the creator dismembered in the act of creating and put back together course by course, and as the year itself. The altar built at Panjal in Kerala over twelve days of April 1975, filmed by Robert Gardner and documented by Staal in the two volumes of Agni (1983), used something over a thousand bricks in its five layers: the performed rite and the ideal rite of the manuals are not the same object, and the difference is one of the most useful things the 1975 recording established.","The altar had to be built to a precise area, and if a second was built it had to have the same area in a different shape — a falcon, a wheel, a trough, a tortoise. That requirement produced the Śulbasūtras, the cord-rules, appendices to the Śrautasūtras attached to the names of Baudhāyana, Āpastamba, Mānava and Kātyāyana and usually placed between the eighth and fifth centuries BCE. They are the earliest Indian mathematical texts. They construct right angles with a marked cord, transform a square into a rectangle or a circle of equal area, and state the theorem of the diagonal — that the square on the diagonal of a rectangle equals the sum of the squares on the two sides — in words, generally, at Baudhāyana 1.48 in Sen and Bag's numbering. They also give a rational approximation to the square root of two accurate to five decimal places. This is the clearest documented case anywhere of a ritual requirement generating a mathematics, and the geometry stayed inside the liturgy for centuries before it went anywhere else.","What the rite was thought to do is the province of Mīmāṃsā, and its answer is startling. Jaimini's Mīmāṃsāsūtra opens by defining dharma as that which is indicated by Vedic injunction (1.1.2) — not as what is good, or what the gods want, but as what the imperative verbs of the Veda command. The fruit of a rite performed today may arrive after death, which leaves a gap between act and result; the school fills it with apūrva, an unseen potency generated by the correct performance and stored until it matures, a doctrine developed in the second book of the Sūtra and at length by Kumārila in the Tantravārttika. The gods, in this account, are not agents who are pleased or displeased. Several Mīmāṃsakas hold that they have no bodies at all and subsist as the words that name them in the mantras. The sacrifice works mechanically, by its own correctness, in a universe with no author — a position considerably more austere than anything the word 'ritual' usually calls to mind.","In 1979 Frits Staal, who had watched the Panjal rite from close quarters, published 'The Meaninglessness of Ritual' in Numen and argued that ritual is pure rule-governed activity, self-contained, with no meaning, function or reference — that the participants attend to the rules and not to any symbolism, and that the syntactic structures of Vedic ritual resemble those of language without carrying anything like semantics. The argument has never been settled. Its critics point out that the Brāhmaṇas consist of almost nothing but the assignment of meanings, that the Mīmāṃsakas built an entire theory of ritual causation, and that the Nambudiri priests themselves objected to being told that what they were doing meant nothing. Objections were raised at the time to other features of the 1975 celebration as well: to the foreign funding and the cameras, and to the substitution of rice-paste effigies for the animal victims, itself defended from within the tradition on the tradition's own grounds. Agnicayanas continue to be performed, at Panjal again in 2011 and elsewhere; śrauta ritualists are few and are not a survival, being trained in the ordinary way by their teachers."],terms:[{native:"यज्ञ",translit:"yajña",gloss:"sacrifice; the ordered offering into fire around which the Vedic literature and the earliest Indian speculation are organised"},{native:"श्रौत",translit:"śrauta",gloss:"pertaining to śruti; the public three-fire ritual of the Śrautasūtras, as against the single-fire domestic rites of the Gṛhyasūtras"},{native:"अग्निचयन",translit:"agnicayana",gloss:"the piling of the fire; the building of a falcon-shaped altar in five layers of moulded brick, treated by the Śatapatha as the reassembly of Prajāpati"},{native:"अपूर्व",translit:"apūrva",gloss:"literally 'not previously existing'; the unseen potency a correctly performed rite generates and stores until its fruit matures — Mīmāṃsā's answer to how an act works after the actor has died"},{native:"ऋत्विज्",translit:"ṛtvij",gloss:"an officiating priest; sixteen serve a soma sacrifice under four chiefs, one of whom, the brahman, says nothing and exists to catch errors"},{native:"शुल्बसूत्र",translit:"śulbasūtra",gloss:"cord-rule; the altar-construction appendices that constitute the earliest Indian mathematics, named for the measuring cord they are worked with"}],texts:[{title:"Śatapatha Brāhmaṇa VI–X",note:"The Agnicayana explained layer by layer, with the altar read as the year and as the dismembered creator; the tenth book, the Agnirahasya, is the most speculative stratum of Vedic prose."},{title:"Baudhāyana Śulbasūtra",note:"Altar geometry, generally placed between the eighth and fifth centuries BCE: constructions of equal area, and the theorem of the diagonal stated in words at 1.48 in Sen and Bag's edition (1983)."},{title:"Jaimini, Mīmāṃsāsūtra, with Śabara's Bhāṣya",note:"Dharma defined at 1.1.2 as what Vedic injunction indicates; the source of apūrva and of the school's near-dispensing with the gods as agents."},{title:"Frits Staal, Agni: The Vedic Ritual of the Fire Altar, 2 vols (1983)",note:"The full documentation of the Panjal performance of April 1975, with texts, photographs, and contributions from participants and observers."},{title:"Frits Staal, 'The Meaninglessness of Ritual', Numen 26 (1979)",note:"The thesis that ritual is rule-governed activity without meaning; contested from the first, and the most productive wrong-or-right argument in the modern study of Vedic ritual."}]},{slug:"mantra",title:"Efficacious Sound",native:"मन्त्र",translit:"mantra",summary:"Whether a word's power sits in its meaning, in its phonemes, or in something the phonemes merely reveal — an argument conducted across fifteen centuries and never resolved.",paragraphs:["A mantra is not a prayer. It is not addressed to anyone who might decline, it does not petition, and its efficacy is held to lie in its sound rather than in its sense — which is why the tradition's most famous cautionary tale is about an accent. The Taittirīya Saṃhitā (2.4.12) tells of a verse spoken to make Tvaṣṭṛ's son the slayer of Indra; misaccented, the compound indraśatru means instead 'he whose slayer is Indra', and the son died accordingly. Patañjali the grammarian retells it in the Paspaśāhnika that opens his Mahābhāṣya, as the argument for why grammar must be studied. Beside this stands the Gāyatrī of Ṛgveda 3.62.10, a single verse on the sun's light which is conferred at Vedic initiation and recited daily by the twice-born, and which is treated as an instrument rather than a statement. What the two examples share is a premise: the sound is the operative thing, and getting it right is not a matter of taste.","Mīmāṃsā turned the premise into a metaphysics. Its position is that śabda, sound, is eternal — that a phoneme is not produced by the speaker but only manifested, so that every utterance of the letter g reveals one and the same imperishable g — and that the relation between word and meaning is autpattika, inborn and not established by anyone (Mīmāṃsāsūtra 1.1.5). From this the Veda's authorlessness follows: no author is remembered for it, no author is required by the theory, and the school holds it apauruṣeya, of no human maker. Nor of a divine one, which is the sharper half of the doctrine — Mīmāṃsā's Veda has no god behind it either. Nyāya took the opposite side: sound is produced, impermanent, and the link between word and meaning is conventional, a convention which later Naiyāyikas assigned to God. Śabara's commentary and Kumārila's Ślokavārttika argue the eternalist case in detail, and the argument is one of the longest-running in Indian philosophy.","Cutting across it is the sphoṭa. Bhartṛhari, the grammarian usually placed in the fifth century CE, opens the Vākyapadīya by identifying the essence of the word with a beginningless, imperishable brahman from which the world unfolds, and defends the position that meaning is not carried by the sequence of phonemes at all: the sounds are a manifestation, and what they manifest is a single indivisible unit, the sphoṭa, a 'bursting' apprehended whole. The term is older — Patañjali uses it, distinguishing the sphoṭa as the word from dhvani as its audible quality — and Maṇḍana Miśra's Sphoṭasiddhi is its classic defence. Kumārila attacks it at length in the Sphoṭavāda section of the Ślokavārttika, holding that eternal phonemes are sufficient and the extra entity superfluous. The alignment repays notice: it is the school that thinks sound eternal which refuses the sphoṭa, and the grammarians, whose whole business is the analysis of phonemes, who insist the meaning does not live in them.","The bīja is where these theories meet the ritual literature. A seed-syllable is typically one closed syllable — a consonant cluster, a vowel, and a nasal resonance — held to contain a deity entire, not to be translatable, and not to be a contraction of anything longer. Hrīṃ, śrīṃ, aiṃ, klīṃ, krīṃ, hūṃ and phaṭ are among the commonest. The mantraśāstra digests systematise them: the Śāradātilaka, ascribed to Lakṣmaṇadeśika and usually placed in the eleventh century, with Rāghavabhaṭṭa's commentary of 1494; the Prapañcasāra; the Mantramahodadhi of Mahīdhara, composed towards the end of the sixteenth century. These works commonly encode their mantras by mantroddhāra, a procedure of coded extraction — the sixth letter of such a class, with the fourth vowel and the crescent above — so that the syllable cannot be read off the page without either the key or a teacher. The doctrine that accompanies this is that a mantra taken from a book is inert, and becomes operative only when conferred at dīkṣā by someone who holds it; the texts describe the transmission, and this hall describes the texts.","The modern argument runs on the same fault line. In Harvey Alper's Understanding Mantras (1989), Staal argued that mantras have no meaning in any ordinary sense and are better compared to birdsong than to sentences, adducing the stobhas — the carrier syllables hāu and hoyi and huvā inserted into Sāmavedic chant — as the pure case: demonstrably meaningless, liturgically indispensable. Contributors to the same volume, Wade Wheelock among them, answered that mantras are speech-acts rather than statements, utterances which do not describe a situation but constitute one, so that asking what they mean is the wrong question rather than a question with the answer 'nothing'. Neither position has carried. What both concede is the tradition's own gradation of utterance, set out at Manu 2.85: the japa sacrifice is ten times the prescribed one, a hundred times if the syllables are whispered, a thousand if they are purely mental — the value rising as the sound becomes less audible, in a discipline that began by insisting the sound was everything."],terms:[{native:"मन्त्र",translit:"mantra",gloss:"an instrument of thought (man- with the suffix -tra); a formula whose efficacy is held to lie in its sound, and which is done rather than said to anyone"},{native:"अपौरुषेय",translit:"apauruṣeya",gloss:"of no human author; Mīmāṃsā's term for the Veda, and a denial of divine authorship as much as of human — the text has no maker at all"},{native:"स्फोट",translit:"sphoṭa",gloss:"a bursting; the indivisible meaning-bearing unit which, on Bhartṛhari's account, the phonemes merely manifest. The Mīmāṃsakas, who hold sound eternal, reject it"},{native:"बीज",translit:"bīja",gloss:"seed; a single-syllable mantra held to contain a deity whole and to be untranslatable, and the germ from which longer mantras are unfolded"},{native:"ह्रीं",translit:"hrīṃ",gloss:"the bīja most often associated with the Goddess, and among the commonest in the Śākta literature; written with the anusvāra that carries its closing resonance"},{native:"जप",translit:"japa",gloss:"the counted repetition of a mantra; Manu 2.85 grades it tenfold over the prescribed sacrifice, a hundredfold whispered, a thousandfold when purely mental"}],texts:[{title:"Jaimini, Mīmāṃsāsūtra 1.1.1–5, with the Śābarabhāṣya",note:"The eternity of sound and the inborn relation of word to meaning; the foundation of the argument that the Veda has no author of any kind."},{title:"Kumārila Bhaṭṭa, Ślokavārttika",note:"Seventh century; the eternalist case argued against Buddhist and Naiyāyika opponents, and, in the Sphoṭavāda section, against the grammarians' extra entity."},{title:"Bhartṛhari, Vākyapadīya, Book I",note:"Usually placed in the fifth century CE; word-essence identified with brahman, and the case for meaning residing in an indivisible whole rather than in the sequence of sounds."},{title:"Śāradātilaka, with Rāghavabhaṭṭa's commentary (1494)",note:"The standard medieval digest of mantraśāstra: the seed-syllables, their coded extraction, and the ritual apparatus that surrounds their conferral."},{title:"Harvey P. Alper (ed.), Understanding Mantras (1989)",note:"The modern argument in one volume — Staal on mantras as meaningless, Wheelock and others on mantras as speech-acts that constitute rather than describe."}]},{slug:"matrka",title:"The Alphabet as Goddess",native:"मातृका",translit:"mātṛkā",summary:"Fifty letters that are fifty goddesses, a skull-garland that is an alphabet, and the closest analogue outside Hebrew to a mysticism of the letters themselves.",paragraphs:["Mātṛkā means little mother, and the word carries two senses that the tradition allows to overlap. One is the group of Mother goddesses — the Seven or Eight Mothers, carved in rows from the Gupta period onward, fierce and collective. The other is the alphabet regarded as Mother: the sounds themselves as a body of deities, and as the matrix out of which mantras and the world are alike composed. The count is fifty, sixteen vowels and thirty-three consonants with the conjunct kṣa added at the end, so that the series runs a to kṣa — a count arrived at for doctrinal rather than phonetic reasons, since the phoneticians of the Prātiśākhyas had no need of kṣa as a unit. The Śiva Sūtras, an aphoristic Kashmiri text of perhaps the ninth century, state the position in three words at 1.4: jñānādhiṣṭhānaṃ mātṛkā, the Mother is the ground of knowledge; and again at 2.7, where the accomplishment named is the full awakening to the wheel of the mātṛkā. Kṣemarāja's commentary in the eleventh century unfolds both.","The Kashmiri Śaiva schools made of this a complete emanationist metaphysics, and it is among the most sustained pieces of speculation about language anywhere. In the third chapter of the Tantrāloka and in the Parātrīśikāvivaraṇa, Abhinavagupta reads the varṇamālā as the phased self-recognition of Śiva: the short a is anuttara, the absolute with nothing beyond it; ā is bliss; the i and u series are will and unfolding; the diphthongs are stages of the will's exteriorisation; and the consonants, in the order the grammarians give them, correspond to the categories of the emanated world from the elements upward. The joint is at the end. A is the first letter and ha the last of the consonants; with the resonance of the anusvāra between them they spell aham, 'I' — so that the alphabet recited from beginning to end is the divine first person uttering itself, and the world is what that utterance says. Whatever one makes of the doctrine, it is not decoration; it is worked out letter by letter with the phonetics of the grammarians assumed throughout.","There are two orders, and the difference between them is technical. Śabdarāśi is the ordinary series, vowels then consonants by place of articulation, the order any Sanskrit child learns. Mālinī, the garlanded, is an irregular sequence running from na to pha in which vowels and consonants are interlaced rather than segregated — traditionally described as the state in which śakti pervades and is pervaded, and taken to be the higher of the two. The Mālinīvijayottara Tantra, whose title announces the victory of Mālinī and which Abhinavagupta takes as the root authority of the Tantrāloka, is the classical source; it was edited by Madhusudan Kaul Shastri in 1922. Different initiations, and different accounts of how sound generates the world, attach to the two orders. That an alphabet should have an esoteric permutation as well as a canonical one is the sort of detail that shows how seriously the ordering was taken.","The letters were also put on the body. Nyāsa is the rite of installing mantras and letters upon the limbs by touch, each syllable spoken as the corresponding place is touched, so that the practitioner's body is rebuilt as the deity's before worship begins; mātṛkānyāsa, which sets the whole fifty on the body in order, is set out in the Śāradātilaka and in the later ritual manuals. The same fifty are distributed around the petals of the six centres in the Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa, and the arithmetic is exact: four petals, then six, ten, twelve, sixteen and two, which sum to fifty. The petal-counts are what they are because the alphabet had to fit. And the Tantras gloss the garland of severed heads that Kālī wears as the muṇḍamālā of the fifty letters — a goddess wearing the alphabet, which is either the most concrete image of letter-mysticism in any tradition or the most literal, depending on how one takes the gloss.","The comparison with Hebrew letter-mysticism is unavoidable and has to be made carefully. Sefer Yetzirah's twenty-two letters as the instruments by which the world is made, the letters distributed over the parts of the body and the divisions of time, the alphabet as a generative set rather than a notation — the structural resemblance to the mātṛkā material is real and specific. What is missing is any demonstrated chain of transmission in either direction. The datings do not help a simple story: Sefer Yetzirah is placed by different scholars anywhere between the second and the sixth centuries, while the developed Kashmiri material is of the ninth to the eleventh, and the proposals of contact that have been offered are speculative. The honest statement is that two literate cultures whose scripts encoded a phonetic analysis arrived at the same idea independently — and that the Indian version had the advantage of a thousand years of descriptive phonetics behind it, since the Prātiśākhyas had classified every sound by place and manner of articulation long before anyone assigned each a metaphysical position."],terms:[{native:"मातृका",translit:"mātṛkā",gloss:"little mother; both the group of Mother goddesses and the alphabet regarded as Mother — a deliberate double sense the Tantras exploit"},{native:"वर्ण",translit:"varṇa",gloss:"a phoneme, a letter; also colour and social class. In the Kashmiri systems each varṇa is a phase of the absolute's self-awareness"},{native:"शब्दराशि",translit:"śabdarāśi",gloss:"the heap of sounds; the ordinary alphabetical order, vowels then consonants by place of articulation, from a to kṣa"},{native:"मालिनी",translit:"mālinī",gloss:"the garlanded one; the esoteric order in which vowels and consonants are interlaced, running na to pha, and the goddess who is that order"},{native:"न्यास",translit:"nyāsa",gloss:"placing; the installation of letters and mantras on the limbs by touch, by which a body is reconstituted as a deity's before worship"},{native:"अक्षर",translit:"akṣara",gloss:"imperishable, and hence syllable; the pun on which the Upaniṣadic treatment of oṃ and the whole doctrine of eternal phonemes turns"}],texts:[{title:"Śiva Sūtras, with Kṣemarāja's Vimarśinī",note:"Seventy-seven aphorisms of the Kashmiri tradition, perhaps ninth century; 1.4 makes the mātṛkā the ground of knowledge, 2.7 the awakening to its wheel, and the eleventh-century commentary supplies the doctrine."},{title:"Abhinavagupta, Tantrāloka, chapter 3",note:"The phonematic emanation worked out letter by letter, with a as the absolute, ha as the last consonant, and aham as what the alphabet spells when it is complete."},{title:"Mālinīvijayottaratantra",note:"Edited by Madhusudan Kaul Shastri, Kashmir Series of Texts and Studies 37 (1922); the source of the interlaced mālinī order and Abhinavagupta's declared root authority."},{title:"Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa",note:"Distributes the fifty letters around the petals of the six centres — 4, 6, 10, 12, 16 and 2, which is fifty exactly. The petal-counts follow from the alphabet, not the other way round."},{title:"André Padoux, Vāc: The Concept of the Word in Selected Hindu Tantras (Eng. tr. 1990)",note:"Originally published in French in 1963; the standard study of speech, phonematic emanation and the mātṛkā in the tantric traditions."}]},{slug:"tantra",title:"Tantra",native:"तन्त्र",translit:"tantra",summary:"Not a doctrine but a family of ritual systems, and a literature so large that what the English word now connotes is a small and late fraction of it.",paragraphs:["Begin with the word. Tantra is from tan-, to stretch, and names first the warp of a loom, then by extension a framework, a system, a manual; the Pañcatantra is five such, on statecraft and prudence, with nothing religious in it. Applied to scripture it names a revealed corpus, but not exclusively: the Śaiva texts call themselves āgama or tantra indifferently, the Vaiṣṇava ones saṃhitā, the Buddhist ones tantra. There is no Sanskrit word for 'Tantrism' and nobody in this history described himself as a Tantrist. What people belonged to were initiatory lineages — a kula, an āmnāya, a santāna — with a particular deity, a particular mantra, a particular set of texts and a particular teacher. To ask what tantra teaches is therefore to ask a question with no single answer, in the way that asking what liturgy teaches would be. It is a body of ritual systems.","The taxonomy that made the field tractable is Alexis Sanderson's. The Atimārga, the outer path — Pāśupatas, Lākulas, the Kālāmukhas — is for ascetics and seeks liberation only. The Mantramārga, the path of mantras, is the tantric literature proper, open to initiated householders as well as ascetics, and offers liberation or supernatural attainments at the initiate's choice. Within it the Siddhānta is dualist, sober in idiom, revealed by Sadāśiva, and its rites became the liturgy of the great South Indian temples; the non-Saiddhāntika streams — the Bhairava tantras and the Śākta systems of the Trika, the Krama, the Kubjikā and Śrīvidyā traditions — bring in the cremation ground, the yoginīs, the fierce goddesses, and the deliberate use of substances the Vedic order excludes. The oldest surviving Śaiva tantra is the Niśvāsatattvasaṃhitā, placed by Dominic Goodall, Sanderson and their collaborators between roughly 450 and 550 CE and known from a single Nepalese palm-leaf manuscript of the ninth century. Sanderson's 'The Śaiva Age' (2009) argues that from the fifth century to the thirteenth the Mantramārga was the dominant religion of the subcontinent's ruling classes — a claim about scale that is easy to miss if one has met tantra only in translation.","What is in the books is mostly ritual. Initiation and its grades; the drawing of maṇḍalas and the measurements for them; the installation and consecration of images and liṅgas; the consecration of kings; daily worship, occasional worship, festival worship; fire offerings; expiation for errors; and, after all that, chapters on yoga and on gnosis. The Śaiva Siddhānta paddhatis are in substantial part temple-building and temple-running manuals, which is why the liturgy performed in a Tamil Śiva temple today is tantric liturgy conducted by initiated priests. Sexual ritual exists in this corpus. It is concentrated in the Kaula streams, it is real, and it is a small share of an enormous literature. David Gordon White's Kiss of the Yoginī (2003) argues that the consumption of sexual fluids was the original core of Kaula practice, later sublimated into the elegant metaphysics of Abhinavagupta's Kashmir; Sanderson and others read the same passages as one ritual among many within systems whose centre of gravity lies elsewhere. The argument is open, and neither side is arguing that the material is not there.","How the English word acquired its meaning is a separate history and worth separating. Tantra entered European usage in the nineteenth century through colonial administration and missionary polemic, attached from the first to precisely the material a Victorian reader would find scandalous, and treated as evidence of a decayed Hinduism. John Woodroffe wrote against that reading from Calcutta in the 1910s and 1920s and did more than anyone to make the texts available; but the polemical category survived him and was refitted. Hugh Urban's Tantra: Sex, Secrecy, Politics, and Power in the Study of Religion (2003) traces the line: from the colonial construction, through Theosophy, through Aleister Crowley's reading, through Pierre Bernard and the American occult scene of the early twentieth century, to the sexual therapeutics marketed under the name from the 1960s onward. Each stage is documented. The distance between the Niśvāsa's chapters on installing a liṅga and a weekend workshop is not a matter of degree, and the word's English history is not evidence about the Sanskrit one.","The evidence has moved fast, because most of this corpus was until recently unpublished and unread. The Nepalese-German Manuscript Preservation Project, running from 1970 to 2002, microfilmed on the order of 180,000 manuscripts, a great many of them tantric, and the palm-leaf survivals of the Kathmandu Valley are the oldest witnesses to much of the Śaiva literature. Critical editions from Pondicherry, Hamburg and Leiden have redrawn the chronology in a single scholarly generation, and the field's own view of what these texts are has changed with it. The traditions are living. Śaiva Siddhānta ritual is performed daily in South Indian temples; Śrīvidyā is transmitted in monastic and householder lineages; the Newar vajrācāryas of Kathmandu maintain a hereditary Buddhist tantric priesthood with no counterpart anywhere else; Śākta worship in Bengal, Assam and Nepal is ordinary religion rather than antiquarianism. Almost all of it is transmitted by initiation, and the texts themselves say so, which is one reason the corpus stayed outside scholarship as long as it did."],terms:[{native:"तन्त्र",translit:"tantra",gloss:"the warp of a loom, thence a system or manual; used of revealed scripture but also of secular treatises, and never as the name of a religion"},{native:"आगम",translit:"āgama",gloss:"that which has come down; the Śaiva scriptures' own commonest self-designation, set against nigama, the Vedic transmission"},{native:"दीक्षा",translit:"dīkṣā",gloss:"initiation; in the Mantramārga a rite held actually to destroy the bonds of the initiate's soul, not merely to admit him to instruction"},{native:"मन्त्रमार्ग",translit:"mantramārga",gloss:"the path of mantras; Sanderson's term, taken from the sources, for the tantric Śaivism of initiated householders and ascetics, as against the ascetic-only Atimārga"},{native:"कुल",translit:"kula",gloss:"family, clan; an initiatory lineage, and the name of the transgressive Kaula streams within which most of the sexual ritual is found"},{native:"पञ्चमकार",translit:"pañcamakāra",gloss:"the five m-words — wine, meat, fish, parched grain and intercourse — used ritually in some Kaula systems, substituted by surrogates in others, and taken by later polemic for the whole of tantra"}],texts:[{title:"Niśvāsatattvasaṃhitā, ed. Dominic Goodall with Alexis Sanderson and Harunaga Isaacson (2015)",note:"The earliest surviving Śaiva tantra, c. 450–550 CE, from a single ninth-century Nepalese palm-leaf manuscript; the base line for everything later."},{title:"Abhinavagupta, Tantrāloka",note:"Thirty-seven chapters synthesising the Trika systems around 1000 CE; the proportion of ritual to metaphysics in it is the best single corrective to what the word tantra now suggests."},{title:"Kulārṇava Tantra",note:"A Kaula work of uncertain date, printed early in Woodroffe's Tantrik Texts series; frank about the transgressive materials and equally frank that they are useless without initiation."},{title:"Alexis Sanderson, 'The Śaiva Age', in Shingo Einoo (ed.), Genesis and Development of Tantrism (2009)",note:"The argument that tantric Śaivism was the dominant religion of the early medieval Indian courts, with the epigraphic and textual evidence set out at length."},{title:"Hugh B. Urban, Tantra: Sex, Secrecy, Politics, and Power in the Study of Religion (2003)",note:"How the English category was assembled out of colonial polemic and then recycled through Theosophy, Crowley and the twentieth-century occult into what is now sold under the name."}]},{slug:"subtle-body",title:"The Subtle Body",native:"सूक्ष्मशरीर",translit:"sūkṣmaśarīra",summary:"Channels, centres and a coiled power — and the historical fact that the seven-cakra scheme the world knows is one system among many, fixed for the West by a single translation of 1919.",paragraphs:["The subtle body is a body that is not the dissected one: a topography of channels, nāḍī, through which prāṇa moves, with junctions or centres strung along a central axis, and a power at the base. Something like its raw material is present very early. The Chāndogya Upaniṣad says at 8.6.6 that a hundred and one channels run from the heart, of which one goes up to the crown, and the Kaṭha repeats it at 6.16; but a count of channels from the heart is not a cakra system, and reading the later scheme back into these passages is the commonest error in the popular literature. The developed systems belong to the tantric traditions, Śaiva and Buddhist, from roughly the eighth century onward, and to the haṭha corpus that follows them. Three channels do most of the work throughout: iḍā and piṅgalā to left and right, associated with moon and sun and with the alternating dominance of the nostrils, and suṣumṇā in the centre, ordinarily closed.","The plurality of schemes is the point a reader is least often told. The Buddhist Hevajra Tantra works with four cakras, at navel, heart, throat and head. The Kubjikāmata, one of the earliest Śaiva sources for a six-centre series, has six. The Netra and Svacchanda tantras give an ascending series of stations that continues past the crown to the dvādaśānta, twelve finger-breadths above the head. The Amṛtasiddhi, the earliest of the haṭha texts, has no cakras of the familiar sort at all: its anatomy is a moon at the palate dripping nectar, a sun at the navel consuming it, and a bindu to be preserved. The late Yoga Upaniṣads count nine, or more. James Mallinson and Mark Singleton set the sources side by side in Roots of Yoga (2017) and the conclusion is not in doubt: the number of centres, their locations, their names, their associated elements and their functions vary by text and by lineage, and no canonical scheme exists in the Sanskrit sources. It is also worth registering what these centres are for in the texts that describe them. They are not psychological zones or emotional registers. They are stations in a ritual and yogic topography — to be pierced, sealed, visualised with a deity resident in them, and passed through.","Kuṇḍalinī has at least three distinct careers. In the Śaiva tantras she is a form of the goddess as the power of speech, coiled in the body as sound is coiled before it is uttered; the Tantrasadbhāva, quoted by Kṣemarāja in the eleventh century, describes her as a sleeping serpent roused by being struck, and the context is phonematic emanation rather than physiology. In the haṭha corpus the treatment is mechanical: kuṇḍalinī is roused by breath-restraint, by the locks and by the seals, and her rise into the central channel serves to stop the nectar of the head from falling into the digestive fire. In the Kashmiri non-dual writers she becomes a name for the movement of consciousness recognising itself. The same word, three theoretical settings, and a modern literature that runs them together as though they were one doctrine described three times.","In 1918 and 1919 the whole of this variety was replaced, for readers in English, by one text. The Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa is fifty-five verses forming the sixth chapter of the Śrītattvacintāmaṇi of Pūrṇānanda, a Bengali Śākta work generally dated to 1577: six centres from mūlādhāra to ājñā, each with its petal-count, element, seed-syllable, presiding deity and śakti, the fifty letters distributed around the petals, and the thousand-petalled sahasrāra above. Sir John Woodroffe, a judge of the Calcutta High Court publishing as Arthur Avalon, translated it with the Pādukāpañcaka and a long introduction as The Serpent Power, from Madras in 1918 and London in 1919. Kathleen Taylor's study of 2001 established that Avalon was a partnership and that the Sanskrit work was largely done by Atal Bihari Ghose, whose name did not appear on the books. Everything downstream descends from this — Jung's seminar of 1932, the Theosophical accounts, the entire modern literature — and usually from the introduction rather than the text. Seven centres, because six plus the crown; a late Bengali text, chosen once, became the tradition.","The colours are later still and are not Indian at all. The Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa does assign colours, but they follow the elements and the letters and form nothing like a spectrum. Kurt Leland's Rainbow Body (2016) traces the actual sequence: Charles Leadbeater's The Chakras (1927) reported colours from what he described as clairvoyant observation, a series of intermediaries adjusted them, and the red-to-violet rainbow ordering that now appears on every chart and every studio wall arrives with Christopher Hills's Nuclear Evolution in 1977. The most widely reproduced diagram of Indian esotericism in the world is thus about fifty years old and was assembled in California. None of this makes the older systems less interesting or the practices less real: they are taught now, under initiation, in Śaiva, Śākta, Buddhist and haṭha lineages, in forms that mostly do not match the chart, and the practitioners are not obliged to reconcile the two."],terms:[{native:"सूक्ष्मशरीर",translit:"sūkṣmaśarīra",gloss:"the subtle body; a Sāṃkhya and Vedānta term for the transmigrating vehicle, borrowed into the yogic literature for the body of channels and centres"},{native:"नाडी",translit:"nāḍī",gloss:"a channel or conduit for prāṇa; counted at 72,000 in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad (2.1.19) and at 350,000 in the Śivasaṃhitā (2.13), of which only three ordinarily matter"},{native:"सुषुम्णा",translit:"suṣumṇā",gloss:"the central channel, ordinarily closed; the practices of the haṭha corpus are largely directed at opening it and forcing the breath into it"},{native:"चक्र",translit:"cakra",gloss:"wheel; a station on the central axis. Four in the Hevajra Tantra, six in the Kubjikāmata and the Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa, nine or more in the late Yoga Upaniṣads — the number is a property of the text, not of the body"},{native:"कुण्डलिनी",translit:"kuṇḍalinī",gloss:"the coiled one; in the Śaiva tantras a form of the power of speech coiled as sound before utterance, in the haṭha texts a physiological agent to be roused by breath and seals"},{native:"बिन्दु",translit:"bindu",gloss:"drop, point; the nasal resonance written above a syllable, and in the haṭha texts the seminal essence whose loss is death and whose preservation is the object of the whole system"}],texts:[{title:"Ṣaṭcakranirūpaṇa",note:"Fifty-five verses forming chapter six of Pūrṇānanda's Śrītattvacintāmaṇi, generally dated 1577; one late Bengali Śākta description among many, and the one the West received."},{title:"Kubjikāmatatantra, ed. Teun Goudriaan and Jan Schoterman (1988)",note:"An early Śaiva source for a six-centre system, and evidence of how such schemes looked several centuries before the text Woodroffe chose."},{title:"The Amṛtasiddhi, ed. and tr. James Mallinson and Péter-Dániel Szántó (2021)",note:"Eleventh century, in a Vajrayāna setting; the earliest text of the haṭha tradition, with an anatomy of moon, sun and bindu and no cakras of the familiar kind."},{title:"James Mallinson and Mark Singleton, Roots of Yoga (2017)",note:"The competing descriptions of the yogic body set side by side in translation; the demonstration that no canonical cakra system exists in the sources."},{title:"Kurt Leland, Rainbow Body: A History of the Western Chakra System from Blavatsky to Brennan (2016)",note:"Traces the rainbow colour scheme to Leadbeater in 1927 and its familiar spectrum ordering to Christopher Hills in 1977 — neither of them a Sanskrit source."}]},{slug:"yoga",title:"Yoga as a Literature",native:"योग",translit:"yoga",summary:"Two hundred aphorisms India largely stopped reading for seven centuries, a separate medieval corpus on breath and seals, and a posture repertoire that does not exist in Sanskrit.",paragraphs:["Yoga is yoking, from the root yuj-, and the word is doing several jobs before Patañjali touches it. The Kaṭha Upaniṣad gives what is often taken as the earliest definition, at 6.11: the steady holding of the senses is what they call yoga — and three chapters earlier it supplies the chariot in which the self is passenger, the intellect driver and the senses horses. The Śvetāśvatara has practical verses on posture and breath at 2.8–13; the Maitrī sets out a six-limbed scheme. The Bhagavad Gītā uses the word for several things at once — action, knowledge, devotion — and its sixth chapter, on meditation, contains the instruction that yoga is not for one who eats too much or too little, sleeps too much or too little. And the same technical vocabulary is in use in the Buddhist and Jain literatures at the same period, which means that priority is genuinely disputed rather than merely uncertain: dhyāna, samādhi, the analysis of mental impressions and their residues belong to the śramaṇa world as much as to the brahmanical one.","The Yoga Sūtras are just under two hundred aphorisms in four chapters. Yoga is defined at 1.2 as the stilling of the turnings of consciousness; the eight limbs are given once, at 2.29; āsana is defined at 2.46 as a posture that is steady and comfortable, and no posture is named anywhere in the text. The third chapter catalogues the powers that accrue — knowledge of past and future, invisibility, entry into another body — and then says at 3.37 that they are obstacles to absorption. The fourth ends in kaivalya, isolation. The text has come down attached to a commentary ascribed to Vyāsa, and Philipp Maas's critical work on the first chapter (2006) argues that sūtra and commentary are one work by one author, the Pātañjalayogaśāstra, composed around 400 CE — in which case the commentator the tradition reveres is Patañjali himself. The tradition, from Bhoja onward, holds something different: that this Patañjali is the grammarian of the Mahābhāṣya, usually placed near 150 BCE, and an incarnation of the serpent Śeṣa, one man given grammar, medicine and yoga, as Bhoja's verse of homage has it. Philology separates them by five and a half centuries and does not attempt to reconcile the accounts.","The commentarial history is thin in an instructive way. Vācaspati Miśra's Tattvavaiśāradī comes around 950; a Vivaraṇa attributed to Śaṅkara survives, published in 1952 and disputed ever since — Paul Hacker argued it is genuinely his, and others have doubted it; Bhoja's Rājamārtaṇḍa is of the eleventh century, Vijñānabhikṣu's Yogavārttika of the sixteenth. Outside Sanskrit there is a remarkable witness: al-Bīrūnī, in India around 1030, translated a recension of the text into Arabic as the Kitāb Pātanjal — a recension that no longer survives in Sanskrit. The manuscript was found in Istanbul by Hellmut Ritter, who published the Arabic in Oriens 9 (1956); Shlomo Pines and Tuvia Gelblum translated it into English in four instalments in the Bulletin of the School of Oriental and African Studies between 1966 and 1989. Then a gap. From roughly the twelfth century to the nineteenth the Yoga Sūtras are little commented on and little cited in India, a fact David Gordon White made the subject of a short book in 2014. Their present eminence begins with the colonial-era revival and with Vivekananda's Raja Yoga of 1896, which presented them to an American audience in a reading of Vivekananda's own.","The haṭha corpus is a separate literature with separate concerns, and the chronology of it was largely rewritten by the Haṭha Yoga Project at SOAS between 2015 and 2020. Its subjects are the preservation of bindu, the seals — mahāmudrā, khecarī, vajrolī — the locks, the six cleansings, breath-restraint, and the raising of kuṇḍalinī. The earliest of these texts is the Amṛtasiddhi, placed in the eleventh century, its oldest dated manuscript of 1160, and its milieu Vajrayāna Buddhist rather than Śaiva — the first text to teach these practices is a Buddhist one, which was not the expected result. The Dattātreyayogaśāstra of the thirteenth century is the first to present haṭhayoga as a named system. The Haṭhapradīpikā of Svātmārāma, mid-fifteenth century, some three hundred and ninety verses compiled from at least twenty earlier works, became the standard manual; it describes about fifteen postures, most of them seated. The Śivasaṃhitā, around 1500, states that there are eighty-four postures and describes four. The Gheraṇḍasaṃhitā, probably late seventeenth century, gives thirty-two. Haṭha means force; the familiar gloss of ha and ṭha as sun and moon is a late etymological reading found in fifteenth-century sources, not the word's sense.","The repertoire of a modern class — the standing sequences, the sun salutation, the linked flowing series — is not in any of this. Norman Sjoman found in the Mysore Palace library a nineteenth-century illustrated āsana section in the Śrītattvanidhi and, beside it, the Vyāyāmadīpikā, a manual of Indian gymnastics in use in the palace gymnasium where Krishnamacharya taught from 1933; his The Yoga Tradition of the Mysore Palace (1996) put the question on the table, and Mark Singleton's Yoga Body (2010) documented the convergence in detail — haṭha technique, Indian physical culture, European and Scandinavian gymnastics, and the physical-education programmes of the YMCA. The argument is frequently misreported as debunking. It is not. Its claim is that modern postural yoga is modern, that its lineages through Krishnamacharya to Iyengar and Pattabhi Jois are real and documented and recent, and that they should not be collapsed into a Sanskrit textual tradition with which they share a vocabulary and rather less else. Both things are true at once, and the hall states both."],terms:[{native:"योग",translit:"yoga",gloss:"yoking, from yuj-; used in the Upaniṣads for restraint of the senses, in the Gītā for several disciplines at once, and by Patañjali for the stilling of mental turnings"},{native:"निरोध",translit:"nirodha",gloss:"stilling, restriction; the operative word of Yoga Sūtra 1.2, and the object of the whole system — not a state to be attained but a cessation"},{native:"आसन",translit:"āsana",gloss:"a seat, thence a posture; defined at Yoga Sūtra 2.46 as steady and comfortable, with no posture named in the text. The Haṭhapradīpikā describes about fifteen, mostly seated"},{native:"प्राणायाम",translit:"prāṇāyāma",gloss:"the extension or restraint of breath; the fourth limb in Patañjali and the operational centre of the haṭha corpus, where it is the instrument rather than a preliminary"},{native:"हठ",translit:"haṭha",gloss:"force, violence; the name of the medieval physical corpus. The reading of ha and ṭha as sun and moon is a late gloss found in fifteenth-century sources, not the word's meaning"},{native:"कैवल्य",translit:"kaivalya",gloss:"isolation, aloneness; the end of Patañjali's system — the seer standing in its own nature, with no union of any kind implied despite the word yoga"}],texts:[{title:"Pātañjalayogaśāstra (the Yoga Sūtras with the Bhāṣya)",note:"Usually placed c. 350–450 CE. Philipp Maas's critical edition of the first chapter (2006) argues that sūtra and commentary are a single work by a single author."},{title:"al-Bīrūnī, Kitāb Pātanjal",note:"An Arabic version made around 1030 of a Sanskrit recension that no longer survives; Arabic text published by Hellmut Ritter, Oriens 9 (1956), English translation by Pines and Gelblum in BSOAS, 1966–1989."},{title:"Svātmārāma, Haṭhapradīpikā",note:"Mid-fifteenth century, about 390 verses compiled from at least twenty earlier works; the standard manual of the medieval physical corpus, and about fifteen postures long."},{title:"The Amṛtasiddhi, ed. and tr. James Mallinson and Péter-Dániel Szántó (2021)",note:"Eleventh century, Vajrayāna milieu, earliest dated manuscript 1160; the earliest text to teach the practices later called haṭha, and not a Śaiva one."},{title:"Mark Singleton, Yoga Body: The Origins of Modern Posture Practice (2010)",note:"The documented twentieth-century history of the modern repertoire; read with Norman Sjoman, The Yoga Tradition of the Mysore Palace (1996), on the Mysore gymnasium and its manuals."}]},{slug:"rasayana",title:"Mercury and the Body",native:"रसायन",translit:"rasāyana",summary:"An Indian alchemy in which gold was the secondary aim; the primary one was a body made incorruptible, and the texts say so in the first chapter.",paragraphs:["Two different things are called rasāyana and confusing them makes nonsense of both. In classical āyurveda it is one of the eight branches of medicine: rejuvenation, treated in the first chapter of the Cikitsāsthāna of the Caraka Saṃhitā, in four sub-chapters of regimen and herbal preparation, including the severe kuṭīprāveśika course in which the patient is secluded for a long period in a purpose-built hut. There is no mercury in it. The mercurial art, rasaśāstra, is a later development which enters the medical literature from the tantric side around the turn of the second millennium and eventually reorganises the pharmacopoeia around metals and minerals. The word rasa carries the strain: juice, sap, essence, taste, the aesthetic savour of the drama theorists — and, in this literature, mercury, simply and without qualification, as though the metal were essence itself.","The principal texts are Śaiva in idiom and appear between the eleventh and the sixteenth centuries. The Rasārṇava, usually placed in the twelfth, is a dialogue of Bhairava and the Goddess, as a tantra is. The Rasahṛdayatantra of Govinda is roughly a century earlier; the Rasendracūḍāmaṇi of Somadeva follows; the Rasaratnasamuccaya, ascribed to a Vāgbhaṭa, is placed variously between the thirteenth and the sixteenth. A Rasaratnākara is ascribed to Nāgārjuna, whom the tradition identifies with the Madhyamaka philosopher of some eight centuries earlier — an identification scholarship does not accept, and one of the clearest cases in this hall of a name being used as a warrant rather than an attribution. The mythology these works open with is consistent and is not ornamental: mercury is the seed of Śiva, sulphur the rajas of the Goddess, mica her issue, so that the operations of the laboratory reproduce a divine intercourse and the vessel is a womb.","The procedures are set out as a fixed sequence of saṃskāras, eighteen of them, through which crude mercury must pass before it is fit for use — sweating, grinding, swooning, raising, precipitating, awakening, restraining, kindling — and the two that carry the most weight are jāraṇa, in which mercury is made to consume mica or gold and thereby gains both weight and potency, and vedha, piercing, the transmutational stroke. The last of the eighteen, śarīrayoga, is its application to a body. Whatever one concludes about the theory, the practice was laboratory work: the texts specify apparatus by name — the swinging dolā, the descending and ascending distillation vessels, the sand bath — and describe the preparation of sulphides, the black amalgam kajjalī, and the calcination of metals into the ash preparations called bhasma. An equipment list is a good test of whether a text is describing something or imagining it, and these texts have one.","David Gordon White drew the distinction that organises the subject in The Alchemical Body (1996): dhātuvāda, the transmutation of base metals into gold, and dehavāda, the transmutation of the body into an immortal one. Unlike much of the Latin corpus, the Indian texts are explicit that the first is subordinate — gold is made to fund the work, or to prove that the mercury has reached the necessary potency, and the goal throughout is the siddhadeha, the accomplished body, which does not decay, and liberation held while still alive. The best external witness is al-Bīrūnī, who reports in his India, written around 1030, that the Hindus have an art resembling alchemy which they call rasāyana, that it is concerned chiefly with the body, and that its practitioners claim by it to restore health and prolong life. He is describing dehavāda accurately and from a distance, which makes his testimony worth more than most.","Rasaśāstra did not end, and this is where a historical account has to be careful in both directions. Mercurial, arsenical and metallic bhasma preparations remain part of the āyurvedic pharmacopoeia, are manufactured in India under drug regulation, and are defended on the tradition's own grounds: that the many stages of processing transform the metal into something that is not the raw toxin. That claim has been tested from outside the tradition. Robert Saper and colleagues, reporting in JAMA in 2004 and again in 2008 on āyurvedic products bought in Boston shops and over the internet, found lead, mercury or arsenic above regulatory limits in about a fifth of the products they sampled in each study. Both facts belong on the record: a continuously practised pharmaceutical tradition with an elaborate theory of detoxification, and an independent analytical literature that keeps finding heavy metals in what reaches the market. Nothing here is a recommendation, and the historical operations are named rather than set out because that is what a description is for."],terms:[{native:"रसायन",translit:"rasāyana",gloss:"the way of rasa; in classical āyurveda a branch of rejuvenative medicine without mercury, in the later literature the mercurial alchemy — two disciplines under one name"},{native:"पारद",translit:"pārada",gloss:"mercury; glossed by the texts as that which carries one across, and identified in their mythology with the seed of Śiva"},{native:"गन्धक",translit:"gandhaka",gloss:"sulphur, literally 'the smelly one'; the counterpart to mercury in the operations and, in the mythology, the Goddess's portion"},{native:"जारण",translit:"jāraṇa",gloss:"digesting; the operation in which mercury is made to consume mica or gold and to gain weight and power by it — the central and most demanding of the eighteen saṃskāras"},{native:"धातुवाद",translit:"dhātuvāda",gloss:"the doctrine of metals; transmutation for gold, explicitly subordinated in the Indian texts to the perfection of the body"},{native:"सिद्धदेह",translit:"siddhadeha",gloss:"the accomplished body; the incorruptible physical body that is the aim of the work, held by its possessor while still alive"}],texts:[{title:"Caraka Saṃhitā, Cikitsāsthāna 1",note:"Rejuvenation as classical medicine understood it — regimens, herbal preparations, and the secluded kuṭīprāveśika course — with no mercury anywhere in it."},{title:"Rasārṇava",note:"Usually placed in the twelfth century; a dialogue of Bhairava and the Goddess in the form of a tantra, and the standard entry point to the mercurial literature."},{title:"Rasaratnasamuccaya",note:"Ascribed to a Vāgbhaṭa and placed between the thirteenth and sixteenth centuries; the most systematic of the digests on apparatus, minerals and the preparation of bhasmas."},{title:"al-Bīrūnī, Kitāb fī taḥqīq mā li'l-Hind (c. 1030), chapter 17 in Sachau's division",note:"An outside observer reporting that the Indian art called rasāyana is directed at the body rather than at metals — independent confirmation of what the Sanskrit texts claim for themselves."},{title:"David Gordon White, The Alchemical Body: Siddha Traditions in Medieval India (1996)",note:"The standard modern study; the source of the dhātuvāda–dehavāda distinction and of the case for reading this literature alongside the tantric and haṭha material rather than apart from it."}]},{slug:"jyotisa",title:"Jyotiṣa",native:"ज्योतिष",translit:"jyotiṣa",summary:"A Vedic calendar science that absorbed Greek horoscopy so completely that the loan words are almost the only surviving evidence of the join.",paragraphs:["Jyotiṣa is a Vedāṅga, a limb of the Veda, and its original employment is calendrical: to determine the days on which rites fall. The Vedāṅgajyotiṣa ascribed to Lagadha survives in two short recensions, thirty-six verses attached to the Ṛgveda and forty-three to the Yajus, and it handles a five-year luni-solar cycle, intercalation, and the position of the solstice. It also contains the line by which the discipline has advertised itself ever since: as the crest is to peacocks and the jewel to snakes, so jyotiṣa stands at the head of the sciences that are limbs of the Veda. Older still are the nakṣatras, the twenty-seven or twenty-eight lunar mansions, whose earliest complete list stands at Taittirīya Saṃhitā 4.4.10 and begins not with Aśvinī but with Kṛttikā, the Pleiades. The dating divides in the usual way. The solstice position the text states corresponds to somewhere around 1400–1200 BCE, and the tradition takes that as its date; philological reckoning places the composition around the fifth or fourth century BCE — Pingree said about 400 — and treats the astronomical datum as an inherited observation preserved in a later text. Both accounts are answering the same question and neither is a paraphrase of the other.","Horoscopic astrology is not Vedic and does not appear in India before the early centuries of the common era. The twelve zodiacal signs, the ascendant, the twelve houses, the aspects, the planetary hours — these came from the Hellenistic world, and the vocabulary is the fossil record of the transfer: horā from ὥρα, kendra from κέντρον, dreṣkāṇa from δεκανός, liptā from λεπτή, jāmitra from διάμετρος, meṣūraṇa from μεσουράνημα, and yavana itself, Ionian, which came to mean Greek and then merely foreign. The Yavanajātaka is the pivot text. David Pingree's edition of 1978 supplied two exact dates — a Greek work turned into Sanskrit prose at Ujjain in 149/150 CE and versified by Sphujidhvaja in 269/270 — and those were repeated as settled for a generation, until Bill Mak's re-examination of the Nepalese manuscript material in 2013 found neither reading supported by what the palm leaves carry. That the transmission happened is not in doubt; when it happened now is. Varāhamihira, in the sixth century, states the position without embarrassment at Bṛhatsaṃhitā 2.15: the yavanas are barbarians, yet this science is well established among them, and for it even they are honoured as seers.","The computational half of jyotiṣa produced mathematics of the first order, and it did so inside the discipline rather than beside it. Āryabhaṭa's Āryabhaṭīya of 499 — he gives his own age as twenty-three when three thousand six hundred years of the Kali Yuga had elapsed — carries a sine table, a value of π given as 62832 over 20000 and expressly labelled approximate, and the statement that the apparent westward motion of the stars is produced by the earth's rotation, a position Brahmagupta rejects in the Brāhmasphuṭasiddhānta of 628 and which was not settled by argument for a millennium. Varāhamihira's Pañcasiddhāntikā summarises five earlier astronomical systems, two of which, the Romaka and the Pauliśa, carry their western origin in their names. Bhāskara II's Siddhāntaśiromaṇi is of 1150. And in Kerala, Mādhava of Saṅgamagrāma, working around 1400, obtained infinite series for π and for the sine and cosine two centuries before Newton and Leibniz; the results are transmitted through Nīlakaṇṭha Somayāji's Tantrasaṅgraha of 1501 and the Yuktibhāṣā. The men who did this work were computing planetary positions for a discipline whose other half was astrology, and they were not two professions.","The discipline divides itself into three. Gaṇita or siddhānta is the computation. Horā is the astrology of the individual, with jātaka for the nativity, praśna for a question asked at a moment, and muhūrta for choosing an auspicious time to act. Saṃhitā is the mundane branch — omens, weather, portents, the fortunes of kingdoms — and Varāhamihira's Bṛhatsaṃhitā, its great compendium, is incidentally one of the best sources anywhere for sixth-century Indian material culture, since a book on omens has to describe architecture, gemstones, perfumery, iconometry and the digging of wells in order to say what is auspicious about them. One major instrument has no Greek counterpart at all: the daśā systems, which divide a life into planetary periods and sub-periods keyed to the position of the moon in a nakṣatra at birth. The Viṃśottarī, on a hundred-and-twenty-year cycle, is the usual one, and its wholly Indian character is a useful corrective to the idea that jyotiṣa is Greek astrology in Sanskrit dress.","One structural difference separates it from Western astrology to this day: Indian practice measures the zodiac against the fixed stars rather than against the equinox, so that its signs and the Western tropical signs have drifted apart by the accumulated precession, at present about twenty-four degrees. The correction is the ayanāṃśa, and which value to use was an unsettled question inside the tradition until the Calendar Reform Committee, chaired by the physicist Meghnad Saha, recommended one in its report of 1955 — since called the Lāhirī ayanāṃśa after N. C. Lahiri, who served on it — for use in the reformed national calendar. Jyotiṣa is not a historical subject. Pañcāṅga almanacs are compiled and published annually; muhūrta is consulted for weddings, journeys and the laying of foundations across much of India and Nepal; astrologers practise as a profession. When the University Grants Commission introduced degree courses in Jyotir Vigyan in 2001, the resulting dispute reached the Supreme Court, which declined in 2004 to strike the decision down. What jyotiṣa is — a science, a limb of the Veda, a superstition — is argued in the present tense by people with a stake in the answer, and this hall records the argument rather than joining it."],terms:[{native:"ज्योतिष",translit:"jyotiṣa",gloss:"the science of the lights; the sixth Vedāṅga, originally calendrical, later covering computation, natal astrology and omens alike"},{native:"नक्षत्र",translit:"nakṣatra",gloss:"a lunar mansion; twenty-seven or twenty-eight divisions of the moon's path, listed at Taittirīya Saṃhitā 4.4.10 beginning with Kṛttikā, the Pleiades"},{native:"होरा",translit:"horā",gloss:"the hour, and thence natal astrology as a branch; a direct loan from Greek ὥρα, and among the plainest evidence of the Hellenistic transmission"},{native:"लग्न",translit:"lagna",gloss:"the ascendant — literally 'attached', the degree of the ecliptic rising at the eastern horizon at the moment in question; the anchor of a horoscope"},{native:"दशा",translit:"daśā",gloss:"a planetary period; the division of a life into ruled spans keyed to the moon's nakṣatra at birth, the Viṃśottarī running 120 years. An Indian development with no Greek antecedent"},{native:"अयनांश",translit:"ayanāṃśa",gloss:"the precessional difference between the sidereal and tropical zodiacs, presently about twenty-four degrees; its standard value was fixed for India by a government committee in 1955"}],texts:[{title:"Vedāṅgajyotiṣa of Lagadha",note:"Two short recensions on the five-year cycle and the solstice. Its stated solstice implies c. 1400–1200 BCE and the tradition dates it accordingly; philology places the composition around the fifth or fourth century BCE."},{title:"Yavanajātaka",note:"Edited by David Pingree in 1978 with dates of 149/150 and 269/270 CE, both undermined by Bill Mak's re-examination of the Nepalese manuscripts in 2013; the Greek transmission is certain, its chronology is not."},{title:"Varāhamihira, Bṛhajjātaka and Bṛhatsaṃhitā",note:"Sixth century; the standard natal manual and the great compendium of omens — the latter also a description of Indian material culture, since an omen book must describe what it judges."},{title:"Āryabhaṭa, Āryabhaṭīya (499 CE)",note:"Sine table, an approximate π expressly labelled as such, and the rotating earth — done inside jyotiṣa, and rejected on that same ground by Brahmagupta in 628."},{title:"David Pingree, Jyotiḥśāstra: Astral and Mathematical Literature (1981)",note:"The survey of the whole field in Gonda's History of Indian Literature, by the scholar whose Census of the Exact Sciences in Sanskrit catalogued its authors and manuscripts."}]}]};export{e as default};
